.Dd @MDOCDATE@
.Dt CLOWN-CHAT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm clown-chat
.Nd clown-owned cross-session chat over the job-wakeup channel
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm clown chat send
.Fl -target Ar session-key | group | '*'
.Brq Fl -message Ar text | Fl -subject Ar subject Op Fl -body Ar body
.Op Fl -from Ar sender-key
.Op Fl -source Ar source
.Op Fl -resource Ar uri
.Nm clown chat read
.Op Fl -peek
.Op Fl -json
.Nm clown chat list
.Op Fl -json
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm clown chat
is clown's cross-session chat surface (RFC-0013 §3), built on the job-wakeup
channel (see
.Xr clown-job 1 ) .
It replaces the chat tools spinclass previously served.
A chat message is a
.Em chat
record \(em a waking, non-terminal type distinct from
.Em message \(em
whose one-line subject is the wake notification and whose full body is stored in
the message's output spool, so a long body never truncates the wake line.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Nm clown chat send
Send a chat message.
.Fl -target
is a session key (a single clown, addressed by its per-instance key), a
.Ev SPINCLASS_SESSION_ID
group label (fans out to every clown under that spinclass session), or
.Ql *
for broadcast.
The message is given one of two ways.
.Fl -message
is the preferred form: a single string in git-commit format \(em a concise
one-line summary, a blank line, then the body \(em which clown splits into the
wake subject (the first line) and the body recovered by
.Nm clown chat read
(the remainder).
A body that follows the summary without an intervening blank line is rejected.
Alternatively, give the two parts explicitly with
.Fl -subject
(the one-line wake notification) and the optional
.Fl -body .
Exactly one of
.Fl -message
or
.Fl -subject
is required, and they are mutually exclusive.
.Fl -from
defaults to this session's key.
.Fl -resource
attaches a resource reference (a
.Li madder://blobs/ Ns Ar digest
URI or any fetchable URI); repeat it to attach several (clown#112).
clown carries the reference only \(em the blob store is external (madder); the
reader fetches the URI itself.
Prints the generated job id.
.It Nm clown chat read
Read chat messages addressed to this session \(em its own (direct), group, and
broadcast channels \(em newer than the read cursor, oldest first, each body
recovered from its spool.
The read advances a per-reader cursor distinct from the wake monitor's, so a
wake never consumes a read and a read never consumes a wake.
Unlike the wake monitor, which suppresses the sender's own messages, it shows
this session's own sends that land on a channel it reads \(em its group,
broadcast, and self-directed sends (conversation history).
A direct send to another session is delivered to that session's channel, not
this one, so it does not appear in your own read; the recipient reads it from
their channel.
Any resource references carried by a message
.Pq Fl -resource No on send
are listed beneath it.
.Fl -peek
reads without advancing the cursor.
.Fl -json
emits one JSON object per message
.Pq Li {job,from,source,scope,subject,body,resources,ts} .
.It Nm clown chat list
List the live chat recipients, grouped by their spinclass session (the
.Ev SPINCLASS_SESSION_ID
decoration).
Each entry shows the recipient's per-instance key and its
.Ev SPINCLASS_DESCRIPTION .
.Fl -json
emits one JSON presence record per line.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev CLOWN_SESSION_ID
The reader's per-instance key; its channel is the direct-message target (see
.Xr clown-job 1 ) .
.It Ev SPINCLASS_SESSION_ID
The group decoration; chat read also services the group channel
.Pq Li ChannelID Ns Pq Ev SPINCLASS_SESSION_ID .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr clown-job 1
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC-0013 (clownfile, per-instance identity, and clown-owned chat) and RFC-0009
(job-wakeup channel).
